p.src:
Now change the structure. Suppose there are three risky variables and two can be controlled: $\begin{aligned}
n&=3, & c&=2, & u&=1,\\
\rho&=\frac{2}{3}.\end{aligned}$ The same number of controlled variables now sits in a different world. Two controlled variables out of six leaves four outside control. Two controlled variables out of three leaves only one. That is why the second case has, in the speaker’s phrase, a pretty good shot.

